{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>And Jacob feared greatly.</b> And if you ask: since angels encountered him, who came to escort him and guard him as we explained in the portion Vayetze, why was he afraid of Esau? One may explain: the angels who encountered him — one was Michael, his prince, and the other Samael, Esau's prince, and therefore <b>and Jacob feared</b>, for just as Michael came to help him, so Samael came to help Esau. <b>And Jacob feared</b> even though he sent him words of appeasement, he feared lest sin had caused him to be delivered into his brother's hand, because he stayed in Laban's house twenty years and did not engage in honoring father and mother, and he thought that Esau's intention was only for evil. Another interpretation: 'we came to your brother, to Esau' — and he did not reply a thing, for he said, 'I myself will go to him, and four hundred men with him, and I will speak with him face to face,' and therefore <b>and Jacob feared</b>, for he did not know his intention, whether for good or for evil." "<b>And it distressed him.</b> The yod is defective (in plene/defective spelling)." "<b>And he divided the people that were with him.</b> Jacob said: if Esau finds me fleeing, I will put hatred in his heart."], :book :genesis, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:07:04.762736830Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}